.TH zprint "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zprint \- GUI print manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzprint [\fIoptions\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zprint -- GUI print manager
Usage:	zprint [ options ]
Keys:	^P/^N select job, ^Z/^V page, Space page down
	p print a file, c cancel job, r reprint / start daemon
.HE
.B zprint
is the print manager of the
.SM COHERENT
GUI.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop.
It watches the
.B lpr
spool directory and shows the queued jobs \- number, a
.B *
on the job now printing, owner, size and the submitting command \-
above a pane describing the selected job (owner, submission time,
banner, notification, and the files to print).
The bar also reports the printer state: printing, jobs held with no
daemon, or idle.
The spool is re-read every 5 seconds.
.B zprint
accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications; see
.BR zview .
.PP
Keyboard commands:
.DS
.ta 6n 22n
	\fB^P, ^N\fR	select the previous / next job
	\fB^Z, ^B\fR	page the description up
	\fB^V, ^F, SPACE\fR	page the description down
	\fBp\fR	the Print button
	\fBc\fR	the Cancel button
	\fBr\fR	the Reprint / Start button
.DE
The buttons:
.IP \fBPrint\fR
asks for the full path name of a file and submits it with
.BR lpr .
.IP \fBCancel\fR
removes the selected job after confirmation.
Cancelling the job now printing tells the printer daemon to abandon
it (the same signal
.B lpskip
sends); cancelling a queued job removes its spool files.
.IP \fBReprint\fR
tells the daemon to start the current job over from the top
(as
.B "lpskip \-r"
does).
When no daemon is running and jobs are held, this button reads
.B Start
instead and starts
.BR /usr/lib/lpd .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 30n
	/usr/spool/lpd	the print spool
	/usr/spool/lpd/dpid	printer-daemon lock file
	/bin/lpr	used by Print
	/usr/lib/lpd	the printer daemon
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zview ,
.B lpr
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Dialog notices:
`Give the full path name of the file',
`Cannot read that file',
`Nothing to print'.
.SH NOTES
The list holds at most 64 jobs.
The spooler's own permissions apply: removing another user's job or
signalling the daemon needs the appropriate user, and such failures
are silent.
